From f762d2ccc716fc6b1e210f4cb10d7aacd788474e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Ron D." <8740514+rgnv@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:16:49 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 18/29] wasm-port: boot the full menu in the browser
 (module-boundary namespacing + client-only boot fallbacks)

The single-image wasm link resolved the menu's FDF parser calls into the game
module's copies of the shared UI surface.  Those copies talk to the game import
table (gi), which is unfilled during a client-only boot, so the first
GlobalStrings.fdf load hung inside a gi-backed read/alloc.  Desktop builds never
saw this: the ELF visibility boundary keeps each module's copies private.

- game.mk: namespace the game module's FDF/UI surface under emscripten
  (~140 symbols: the parser machinery incl. macro-generated frame parsers, the
  host services, and the theme accessors all move behind W3G_/UI_Game_ names).
- hud.c / hud_write.c: gi-backed FDF host services fall back to engine-level
  reads/allocs when the import table is not filled yet; texture/model loads
  no-op instead of jumping through a null gi table.
- menu_main.c: the browser menu FDF batch is no longer deferred -- the wedge it
  worked around is fixed by the namespacing.
- stb_fdf.h: MAX_UI_CLASSES 2048 -> 8192 (one shared frame table across modules
  in the wasm image; the full menu batch exceeds the old cap).
- cl_movie.c: browser movie playback -- the page owns decoding (fullscreen
  <video> overlay); WC3_MovieDone resumes the engine.
- cl_input.c: pointer-capture handling for the emscripten input path.
